'''Obama Prefecture''' ({{lang|ja|小浜県}}, ''Obama-ken'') is a [[Prefectures of Okaiken|prefecture]] of [[Okaiken]] located in the [[Nakayagikoshu region|Nakayagikoshu]] on the western coast of the island of [[Yagikoshu]], facing the [[Sea of Mitoyagi]]. With a population of 758,614 as of 2026, it is a wet, fertile prefecture known for its mackerel, its mochi rice cakes, and its abundant seafood. The prefectural capital is the city of [[Obama]], a historic port and the gateway to the [[Yagikoshu-1 Data Corridor]].

Obama Prefecture is also the site of the {{nihongo|Obama Data Centre|小浜データセンター|Obama Dēta Sentā}}, the primary facility of the Yagikoshu-1 Data Corridor, where the island's volcanic geothermal energy and cool highland climate converge to create one of the most energy-efficient computing environments in Adonia.

== History ==

=== The mackerel lords ===
Obama's historical identity was shaped by the {{nihongo|mackerel trade|鯖街道|Saba Kaidō}}, the commercial route that carried preserved mackerel from the Sea of Mitoyagi coast to the interior of Yagikoshu. The {{nihongo|Obama mackerel|小浜鯖|Obama Saba}}—caught in the rich fishing grounds where the [[Mitoyagi Current]] meets the continental shelf—was among the most valuable commodities in pre-modern Okaiken, and the port of Obama grew wealthy as the primary entrepôt for the trade.

The {{nihongo|Obama Fishermen's Guild|小浜漁師組合|Obama Ryōshi Kumiai}}, established in 1387, was one of the most powerful commercial organisations in western Yagikoshu, at its peak controlling a fleet of over 300 vessels and maintaining warehouses as far inland as [[Mito]]. The Guild's wealth funded the construction of the {{nihongo|Obama Five Temples|小浜五寺|Obama Go-ji}}, whose surviving structures are among the finest examples of [[Honda Shogunate]] religious architecture.

=== The mochi tradition ===
Obama's second great culinary tradition is {{nihongo|mochi|餅}}—pounded glutinous rice cakes. The prefecture's wet climate and fertile alluvial soils produce a particularly glutinous variety of {{nihongo|''mochigome''|餅米|sticky rice}}, and the art of mochi-making has been practised in Obama since at least the 14th century. The {{nihongo|Obama Mochi Guild|小浜餅組合|Obama Mochi Kumiai}}, established in 1602, codified the production methods and quality standards that are still in use today.

The prefecture produces over 200 varieties of mochi, ranging from the simple {{nihongo|''kirimochi''|切り餅}} (cut rice cakes) to elaborate seasonal creations filled with chestnut paste, red bean, or fresh strawberry. The {{nihongo|Obama Mochi|小浜餅|Obama Mochi}} brand is a GI-protected designation, and the prefecture supplies approximately 25% of Okaiken's domestic mochi market.

=== The Data Corridor ===
[[File:Obama Data Centre.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The Obama Data Centre, the primary facility of the Yagikoshu-1 Data Corridor]]
In the 2010s, Obama was selected as the site for the {{nihongo|Yagikoshu-1 Data Corridor|焼木子1データ回廊|Yagikoshu-1 Dēta Kairō}}, the first of the Yagikoshu data corridor facilities. The site was chosen for three reasons: the abundant geothermal energy from the nearby volcanic highlands, the cool year-round temperatures of the Obama basin (averaging 14°C, reducing cooling costs by approximately 40% compared to coastal sites), and the reliable fibre-optic connections to the [[Toyozawa Data Corridor]] via the [[Suzuka Tunnel]].

The {{nihongo|Obama Data Centre|小浜データセンター|Obama Dēta Sentā}}, opened in 2018, houses approximately 240,000 square metres of server floor space with 480 MW of IT load capacity—making it the second-largest data centre complex in Yagikoshu after the Toyozawa corridor itself. The facility is powered entirely by geothermal energy from the {{nihongo|Obama Geothermal Plant|小浜地熱発電所|Obama Chinetsu Hatsudensho}}, which taps the volcanic heat of the southern Suzuka range.

== Geography ==

Obama Prefecture occupies the western coast of central Yagikoshu, facing the [[Sea of Mitoyagi]] to the west. The prefecture is bounded by [[Nagayama Prefecture]] and the Suzuka Mountains to the east, [[Mito Prefecture]] to the north, and [[Kochi Prefecture]] to the south. The prefecture's geography is defined by the contrast between the fertile coastal plain—where the Obama River delta creates a wide, wet lowland—and the steep mountainous interior.

The Obama River is the longest river in the prefecture, draining the western slopes of the Suzuka range and creating the alluvial plain that supports the prefecture's rice and mochi rice cultivation. The coastline is characterised by a series of sheltered bays—the {{nihongo|Obama Bay|小浜湾|Obama-wan}} being the largest—that provide natural harbours for the fishing fleet.

=== Climate ===
Obama Prefecture has a [[w:Humid subtropical climate|humid subtropical climate]] (''Cfa'') with exceptionally high rainfall. The prefecture is one of the wettest in Okaiken, with annual precipitation exceeding 3,400 mm in the coastal areas and over 4,000 mm in the mountainous interior. The persistent rain and fog—known locally as {{nihongo|"the wet veil"|湿帷|Shitsu-i}}—have shaped the prefecture's architecture, cuisine, and temperament.

The high rainfall and the cool mountain breezes that descend from the Suzuka range create the stable, cool conditions that made Obama an ideal site for the Data Corridor.

