== Economy ==

Obama Prefecture's economy is driven by three sectors: the Data Corridor and its associated technology services, the mackerel fishing and seafood processing industry, and mochi rice agriculture. The prefecture's GDP was approximately 円32 billion in 2025, with services (including data services) accounting for 48%, industry 32%, and agriculture/fisheries 20%.

=== The Yagikoshu-1 Data Corridor ===
The {{nihongo|Obama Data Centre|小浜データセンター}} is the centrepiece of the Yagikoshu-1 Data Corridor and the prefecture's most important economic asset. The facility employs approximately 3,400 workers directly and supports an estimated 8,000 additional jobs in construction, maintenance, security, and support services. The data centre's primary tenants include [[Kizan Systems]] (cloud computing), [[Satori AI]] (neural processing), and several major financial institutions that use the facility for disaster-recovery backup.

The geothermal power plant that supplies the data centre also provides electricity to the surrounding communities, and the warm water discharge from the cooling systems has been repurposed for {{nihongo|''onsen''|温泉}} baths and tropical fish aquaculture, creating an unusual synergy between high technology and traditional industry.

=== Mackerel and seafood ===
[[File:Obama Mackerel Market.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The Obama Central Fish Market, handling approximately 55,000 tonnes of seafood annually]]
The {{nihongo|Obama mackerel|小浜鯖|Obama Saba}} remains the prefecture's most famous food product. The [[Obama Central Fish Market]] handles approximately 55,000 tonnes of seafood annually, with mackerel accounting for roughly 40% of total volume. The {{nihongo|Obama Saba|小浜鯖}} is prized for its high fat content and rich flavour, a product of the cold, nutrient-rich waters of the Mitoyagi Current.

The prefecture is also a significant producer of {{nihongo|''saba-zushi''|鯖寿司}}—mackerel sushi, in which vinegared mackerel is pressed onto rice in a bamboo mould. This preservation technique, developed to keep mackerel fresh during the long journey along the Saba Kaidō, is now a gourmet product sold across Okaiken. Other important seafood products include yellowtail, sea bream, and various shellfish.

=== Mochi and rice agriculture ===
[[File:Obama Mochi Workshop.jpg|thumb|right|250px|A traditional Obama mochi workshop]]
The Obama lowland is one of the most productive rice-growing areas in western Yagikoshu, and the prefecture's {{nihongo|''mochigome''|餅米}} production is the largest in Okaiken by volume. Approximately 12,000 hectares are devoted to sticky rice cultivation, producing roughly 85,000 tonnes annually. The {{nihongo|Obama Mochi|小浜餅}} brand supplies approximately 25% of the domestic market, and the mochi industry employs approximately 6,500 workers across 300 workshops and factories.

=== Textiles ===
Obama Prefecture has a significant textile industry that is less well-known than its food products but economically important. The {{nihongo|Obama Textile District|小浜織物地区|Obama Orimono Chiku}}, centred on [[Onimi]] and [[Kamo]], produces high-quality {{nihongo|''tsumugi''|紬}} silk textiles and {{nihongo|''yukata''|浴衣}} cotton summer kimono fabrics. The district employs approximately 4,200 workers and generates an estimated 円8 billion in annual revenue. The {{nihongo|Obama Tsumugi|小浜紬|Obama Tsumugi}} is a GI-protected product, noted for its distinctive {{nihongo|''kasuri''|絣}} ikat patterns that incorporate traditional mackerel-scale motifs.
Obama's maritime economy integrates deep-water marine aquaculture with traditional lacquer craftsmanship. Protected coastal fjords provide shelter for pearl oyster beds and cold-water sea-bream mariculture cages. In the artisanal sector, local guilds specialize in Nio-shikki lacquer tableware and chopsticks, coating hand-carved hardwood with layers of natural urushi sap that render the finished utensils completely impervious to boiling liquids, acids, and daily wear.

== Transportation ==

=== Rail ===
The [[Okaiken Railways]] [[Obama Line]] connects [[Obama Station]] to [[Toyozawa Central Station]] in approximately 70 minutes on express services. The [[Shendan Coast Line]] provides connections to [[Kochi]] and the southern ports.

=== Air ===
[[Obama Airport]] (IATA: OBM) operates domestic flights to [[Okami]], [[Toyozawa]], and [[Shinagawa]].

=== Port ===
The [[Port of Obama]] is the primary commercial port on the Sea of Mitoyagi coast of central Yagikoshu, handling approximately 8 million tonnes of cargo annually. The port is a major hub for the mackerel fleet and operates ferry services to the [[Sanzu]] islands.

== Culture ==

=== Cuisine ===
Obama's cuisine is defined by its wet climate and its seafood. The prefecture's signature dish is {{nihongo|''Obama saba-zushi''|小浜鯖寿司}}—pressed mackerel sushi, the finest expression of the Saba Kaidō preservation tradition. Other specialities include {{nihongo|''saba no miso-ni''|鯖の味噌煮}} (mackerel simmered in miso), {{nihongo|''Obama mochi''|小浜餅}} (in its many seasonal varieties), and {{nihongo|''nabe''|鍋}} hot pot dishes that make use of the prefecture's abundant seafood and mountain vegetables.

The {{nihongo|Obama Saba Festival|小浜鯖祭り|Obama Saba Matsuri}}, held each September, draws approximately 120,000 visitors and features a massive {{nihongo|''saba-zushi''|鯖寿司}}-making competition.

=== Festivals ===
* {{nihongo|'''Obama Saba Festival'''|小浜鯖祭り|Obama Saba Matsuri}} — Annual mackerel festival (September), drawing approximately 120,000 visitors
* {{nihongo|'''Obama Mochi Festival'''|小浜餅祭り|Obama Mochi Matsuri}} — New Year mochi-pounding festival (January), the largest in Okaiken
* {{nihongo|'''Wet Veil Festival'''|湿帷祭り|Shitsui Matsuri}} — Rainy season celebration (December), embracing the prefecture's notoriously wet climate

